====Etymology====
An {{obor|zh|ja|-|nocap=1}} {{ja-r|硅%素|けい%そ}} ({{zh-ref|Wu, 2010}}); pronunciation from its phonetic part {{zh-l|圭}} ([[ibid.]]), in turn from  {{der|zh|nl|keiaarde|'''kei'''aarde|silicon}}. The usage of {{zh-l|矽}} is dated in Mainland China as it is homophonous to {{zh-l|硒}} "selenium" and {{zh-l|錫}} "tin". In Taiwan, these three words are not homophonous.

The 1932 ROC Ministry of Education's ''Principles of Chemical Nomenclature'' says:
: {{zh-x|硅 由 日 名 珪素 孳 演 而 成，因 爲{wéi} 固體，故 改 王 旁 爲{wéi} 石。|<big>'''硅'''</big> is derived and developed from the Japanese name {{ja-l|珪素|<small>けいそ, keiso</small>}}. Considering Si is a solid substance, we hence convert the component 王 into 石.|CL|collapsed=y|ref='''1932''', {{lang|zh|國立編譯館}}, {{lang|zh|化學命名原則}}}}

